“A Kubernetes LoadBalancer exposes a single Service externally at Layer-4, which works well for simple use cases. However, in a production environment with multiple services, using a LoadBalancer is not suitable because it requires a separate cloud LoadBalancer for each service, making the setup more expensive and difficult to manage.
Ingress addresses this problem by providing Layer-7 traffic management. In practice, we deploy a single external LoadBalancer in front of an Ingress Controller such as NGINX or AWS ALB, and define Ingress rules to route traffic to multiple backend services based on hostnames or URL paths.
An Ingress Controller continuously watches Ingress resources, and when rules are created or updated, it automatically configures the underlying LoadBalancer or proxy to route traffic accordingly.
Ingress also enables centralized TLS termination, cleaner HTTP routing, better scalability, and significant cost optimization, which is why it is the preferred approach in production-grade Kubernetes architectures.”
